{%- comment -%}
  Shared post listing — used by the home page, /archive/ and every tag page.
  Callers assign a `post_list` variable first, then include this file; the
  include sees the caller's scope.

  TWO ROW STYLES, chosen by the caller:

    * FEATURED — title + multi-line body teaser + inline tag pills, date on
      the right. The rich row.
    * COMPACT  — title + date on one line. Nothing else.

  The caller sets `featured_count` to say how many of the leading posts get
  the rich treatment; everything after that is compact. Unset means 0, i.e.
  every row compact, which is what a full index (/archive/, tag pages) wants.
  The home page sets 5.

  Reading `featured_count` takes two steps for a reason. Cobalt's Liquid
  raises "Unknown variable" the moment an unset name is *used in an
  expression* — a `default: 0` filter never gets to run, because the lookup
  fails first. A conditional TEST of a missing name is tolerated (reads
  falsy), so that is the only safe way to ask whether the caller set it. Same
  rule the paginator guards in tags.liquid rely on.

  Only the title is a link. The whole-row stretched link from slice 3c was
  reverted in 3d — see _sass/_posts.scss.

  NOTE: do not write raw Liquid tag syntax inside a comment block. Cobalt's
  Liquid still counts block tags in there, so a conditional written out
  longhand opens a block that never closes and the build dies with
  "Unclosed block".
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign featured_cutoff = 0 %}
{%- if featured_count %}{% assign featured_cutoff = featured_count %}{% endif %}
<ul class="posts-list">
  {%- for post in post_list %}
  {%- if forloop.index0 < featured_cutoff %}
  <li class="post-row post-row--featured">
    <div class="post-item">
      <a class="post-title" href="/{{ post.permalink }}">{{ post.title }}</a>

      {%- comment -%}
        The teaser is drawn from the post BODY, not from excerpt. excerpt is
        only the first paragraph (excerpt_separator defaults to a blank line),
        which collapses to a single line when a post opens with a short one —
        too little to decide whether to click. content is the whole rendered
        post, so flatten it to text and take the opening of it instead.

        42 is tuned, not arbitrary: Liquid's own word count runs a few ahead of
        a plain whitespace count, so 42 here renders as 43-45 actual words —
        mid-range for the 40-50 target, three or four lines on screen.

        Newlines survive strip_html, but HTML collapses them, so the result
        reflows as normal prose. One rough edge: flattening runs a heading's
        text straight into the paragraph before it, with no punctuation
        between. Only shows up on posts whose first 45 words reach a heading.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- assign preview = post.content | strip_html | strip %}
      {%- if preview != "" %}
      <p class="post-excerpt">{{ preview | truncatewords: 42, "…" }}</p>
      {%- endif %}

      {%- comment -%}
        A second, explicit way into the post, under the teaser.

        Deliberately OUTSIDE the excerpt guard above: a featured row always
        offers this, including for a post whose body flattened to nothing and
        therefore has no teaser to read.

        Same href expression as the title, so the two anchors in this row
        always resolve to one URL — if the permalink pattern ever changes,
        both move together. They are plain siblings, not nested: the stretched
        link from 3c is long gone and nothing overlays the row, so both are
        ordinary focusable anchors.

        The arrow is written as an entity to match the ones already in
        tags.liquid and .post-footer.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <a class="post-more" href="/{{ post.permalink }}">View this post &#8594;</a>

      {%- comment -%}
        Test .size, not truthiness: Liquid treats an empty array as truthy, so
        testing the array alone renders an empty tag list for untagged posts.
        slugify matches how Cobalt names the generated tag directories
        ("Home Lab" becomes tags/home-lab/), so these links stay correct for
        multi-word tags.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if post.tags.size > 0 %}
      <ul class="tag-list">
        {%- for tag in post.tags %}
        <li><a class="tag" href="/tags/{{ tag | slugify }}/">{{ tag }}</a></li>
        {%- endfor %}
      </ul>
      {%- endif %}
    </div>

    <span class="post-date">{{ post.published_date | date: "%B %d, %Y" }}</span>
  </li>
  {%- else %}
  {%- comment -%}
    No .post-item wrapper here on purpose: with only two children the <li>'s
    own flex rule puts title and date on one line, which is exactly the
    original listing markup.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <li class="post-row post-row--compact">
    <a class="post-title" href="/{{ post.permalink }}">{{ post.title }}</a>
    <span class="post-date">{{ post.published_date | date: "%B %d, %Y" }}</span>
  </li>
  {%- endif %}
  {%- endfor %}
</ul>
